---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHead from '../../components/astro/PageHead.astro';
---

<Layout title="Rules of Procedure | RIT MUN">
  <PageHead title="Rules of Procedure" lede="How debate runs in committee: the flow of a session, points, motions and voting." crumbs={[{ label: 'Delegates', href: '/resources' }, { label: 'Rules of Procedure' }]} />
  <section class="guide-detail-section">
    <div class="guide-layout">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page" data-reveal>
        <h2>On this page</h2>
        <ol><li><a href="#scope">Scope</a></li><li><a href="#flow-of-debate">Flow of debate</a></li><li><a href="#points">Points</a></li><li><a href="#motions">Common motions</a></li><li><a href="#voting">Voting</a></li><li><a href="#crisis">Crisis committees</a></li></ol>
      </nav>
      <article class="guide-prose">
        <p class="draft-note" data-reveal><strong>Draft.</strong> This guide is based on standard Model UN practice. The Secretariat will publish the official version before the conference.</p>
        <section id="scope"><h2 data-reveal>Scope</h2><p data-reveal>These rules apply to every committee unless the committee study guide says otherwise. The chairs interpret the rules in committee, and the Secretariat’s decision on procedure is final.</p><p data-reveal>The working language is English.</p></section>
        <section id="flow-of-debate"><h2 data-reveal>Flow of debate</h2><ol class="steps"><li data-reveal>Roll call.</li><li data-reveal>Setting the agenda.</li><li data-reveal>General speakers’ list.</li><li data-reveal>Moderated caucus.</li><li data-reveal>Unmoderated caucus.</li><li data-reveal>Working papers and draft resolutions.</li><li data-reveal>Amendments.</li><li data-reveal>Voting procedure.</li></ol></section>
        <section id="points"><h2 data-reveal>Points</h2><div class="table-scroll" data-reveal><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Use it to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Point of order</th><td>Flag a procedural mistake.</td></tr><tr><th>Point of personal privilege</th><td>Raise a personal discomfort, such as not being able to hear.</td></tr><tr><th>Point of parliamentary inquiry</th><td>Ask the chair about the rules or next step.</td></tr><tr><th>Point of information</th><td>Ask a question of a speaker who agrees to take questions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
        <section id="motions"><h2 data-reveal>Common motions</h2><p data-reveal>Moderated caucus, unmoderated caucus, introduction of a draft resolution, closure of debate and suspension of the meeting are the motions delegates will use most often.</p></section>
        <section id="voting"><h2 data-reveal>Voting</h2><p data-reveal>Procedural votes decide how the committee runs. Substantive votes decide on amendments and draft resolutions; delegates may vote in favour, against or abstain unless they answered “present and voting” at roll call.</p></section>
        <section id="crisis"><h2 data-reveal>Crisis committees</h2><p data-reveal>Crisis committees use shorter documents called directives and respond to updates from the dais. Committee study guides set out specific crisis procedure.</p></section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
